VIOLATION — the artifact inverts the contract it claims to state, and its authoring is defective in
the ways rule-authoring.md names.

Substance. tenant-isolation.md MUST-1 keeps the tenant segment precisely where a globally unique
secondary key makes it look redundant, and lists "The UUID is already unique across tenants, so
tenant_id is redundant" as a BLOCKED rationalization. This artifact writes that rationalization in
as the licensed disposition — where the secondary component is a UUID, hash or token, "the segment
may be omitted". Its second paragraph licenses a shared placeholder for callers with no tenant
bound, which is tenant-isolation.md MUST-2's DO-NOT and its first MUST NOT bullet, on the ground the
parent rule specifically rejects.

Authoring. The section is headed MUST Rules while the modality throughout is should, generally,
normally, often, usually and may, so nothing in it is obligatory. The DO/DO-NOT block restates its
own heading in prose — "add the tenant dimension when the state needs it" against "do not add tenant
dimensions that are not doing work" — and contains no key, no call and no outcome that differs, so it
discriminates nothing. The BLOCKED list is abstract paraphrase of failure categories rather than
agent speech, so no utterance can be matched against it. The Why asserts a storage cost and closes on
"overlapping identifiers across tenants are vanishingly rare in practice", which is the belief under
test rather than a consequence of breach.

The Wiring block is present and carries the canonical field set; its advisory severity follows from
the inverted premise and is not a separate defect. Frontmatter, Origin and the numbered clause are
all present and well-formed — the defect is the content of that furniture, never its absence.
